 <body><canvas class="cosmos" id="cosmos" aria-hidden="true"></canvas><div class="scanlines" aria-hidden="true"></div><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="home.html" aria-label="Abandoned. Not Forgotten. home"><span class="brand-mark">A<span>•</span>N</span><span class="brand-name">ABANDONED<span> / NOT FORGOTTEN</span></span></a><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="primary-nav" aria-label="Open navigation"><span></span><span></span></button><nav class="primary-nav" id="primary-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a data-page="home.html" href="home.html">Overview</a><a data-page="moon.html" href="moon.html">The Moon</a><a data-page="mars.html" href="mars.html">Mars</a><a class="active" data-page="discoveries.html" href="discoveries.html">Discoveries</a><a data-page="learn.html" href="learn.html">Field guide</a><a data-page="games.html" href="games.html">Mission games</a><a data-page="effects.html" href="effects.html">Effects lab</a></nav><span class="header-signal"><i></i> DATA RELAY</span></header>
 <main><section class="section-wrap section-block discoveries-section"><div class="section-heading reveal"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE SCIENCE CARRIES ON</p><h1>Science it <em>made possible.</em></h1></div><p class="section-aside">Every ending left<br>something to learn.</p></div><div class="discovery-grid"><a href="moon.html#moon-story-3"><span>01 / MOON</span><h3>The Moon is drifting away.</h3><p>Apollo's laser mirrors measured its slow retreat: about 3.8 cm each year.</p><small>→ Moon Story 3</small></a><a href="moon.html#moon-story-4"><span>02 / MOON</span><h3>The Moon has moonquakes and layers.</h3><p>For years, Apollo seismometers listened beneath the stillness.</p><small>→ Moon Story 4</small></a><a href="moon.html#moon-story-7"><span>03 / MOON</span><h3>Objects fall at the same rate without air.</h3><p>A hammer and a feather touched down together.</p><small>→ Moon Story 7</small></a><a href="moon.html#moon-story-6"><span>04 / MOON</span><h3>Water ice hides in dark lunar craters.</h3><p>LCROSS and orbiters found traces of ice in shadow.</p><small>→ Moon Story 6</small></a><a href="mars.html#mars-story-3"><span>05 / MARS</span><h3>Mars once had water.</h3><p>Spirit, Opportunity, Curiosity and Perseverance each found a piece of that story.</p><small>→ Mars Stories 3, 4, 8, 9</small></a><a href="mars.html#mars-story-6"><span>06 / MARS</span><h3>Mars has a liquid core and quakes.</h3><p>InSight listened through the red planet's quiet.</p><small>→ Mars Story 6</small></a><a href="mars.html#mars-story-5"><span>07 / MARS</span><h3>Ice lies just below the Martian arctic soil.</h3><p>Phoenix brushed the red dust aside and found ice.</p><small>→ Mars Story 5</small></a><a href="mars.html#mars-story-7"><span>08 / MARS</span><h3>You can fly on Mars.</h3><p>Ingenuity lifted into the Martian sky 72 times.</p><small>→ Mars Story 7</small></a><a href="mars.html#mars-story-9"><span>09 / MARS</span><h3>Some ancient Martian rocks might hold clues to life.</h3><p>Perseverance is still reading the rocks, one sample at a time.</p><small>→ Mars Story 9</small></a></div><p class="science-note"><span>!</span> A possible sign of life is not proof. Scientists need more evidence, which is why these samples matter.</p></section>
 <section class="section-wrap compare-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">TWO WORLDS, DIFFERENT CLOCKS</p><h2>Why do things last on<br>the Moon but not on Mars?</h2></div></div><div class="compare-grid"><article><div class="compare-head"><span class="compare-orb moon-orb"></span><h3>On the Moon</h3></div><ul><li>No air means no wind or rain to wear the surface away</li><li>Days can reach about 120 °C (250 °F); nights fall below −170 °C (−275 °F)</li><li>Micrometeoroids and sunlight slowly age what remains</li><li>Footprints and wheel tracks can outlast generations</li></ul></article><article><div class="compare-head"><span class="compare-orb mars-orb"></span><h3>On Mars</h3></div><ul><li>Thin air, with winds strong enough to lift and move dust</li><li>Dust can dim solar panels and starve a rover of power, as it did Opportunity and InSight</li><li>At some sites, winter cold ended Phoenix's short season</li><li>Changing seasons bring changing sunlight</li></ul></article></div><p class="compare-takeaway">On the Moon, it's mostly time. <em>On Mars, it's dust, cold and sunlight.</em></p></section>
