 <main><section class="world-section mars-section page-world"><div class="world-banner reveal"><div class="world-symbol mars-symbol" aria-hidden="true"></div><div><p class="eyebrow">WORLD 02 / 225M KM AVG. DISTANCE</p><h1>Mars: <em>Robots in the Dust</em></h1><p>Mars lies farther away beneath a thin, dusty sky. No human has walked there; every mark in this archive was made by a machine. Some explorers lasted months. Others outlived their plans by years. Two are still at work.</p></div><span class="world-code">ARES<br>02</span></div><div class="story-grid" id="mars-stories"></div></section><section class="section-wrap failure-section"><div class="failure-marker"><span>⚪</span><b>LOST SIGNAL</b><i>1999</i></div><div><p class="eyebrow">WHEN THINGS GO WRONG</p><h2>Not every mission<br>finds its way home</h2><p>In 1999, two Mars spacecraft were lost. Mars Climate Orbiter disappeared because two teams used different units: one metric, one not. Mars Polar Lander fell silent while trying to land. Both losses taught engineers to check every number twice, and helped make later journeys safer.</p></div></section><section class="section-wrap page-next story-handoff"><p class="eyebrow">CHAPTER 03 / WHAT THE JOURNEY LEFT US</p><h2>The machines go quiet.<br><em>Their discoveries don't.</em></h2><p>From moonquakes to ancient water, each signal returned with a new piece of the story.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="discoveries.html">Follow the discoveries <span>→</span></a></section></main>
